  "ai_show_review": {
    "summary": "Rochester ’82 delivers fire‑filled Bird Song, China Cat > Rider and a sweet Brokedown Palace.",
    "review": "April 9 1982 at the Community War Memorial in Rochester is one of those high‑energy spring shows that still feels fresh. The band bursts out of the gate with “Alabama Getaway” and rides a relentless wave through classics – a soaring, almost blistering Bird Song, a smoking China Cat > I Know You Rider, and an emotionally charged To Lay Me Down that showcases Brent’s bright organ harmonies. Set two is a jam‑fest: drums and space swell into a powerful The Other One, then melt back into the plaintive beauty of Stella Blue before closing with a rousing Satisfaction and a sweet Brokedown Palace encore. Across all sources the performance is tight yet adventurous – Jerry’s lead work is crisp and inspired, Phil anchors the improvisations with his trademark bass thump, Bob delivers solid vocals, Brent adds frenzied organ textures, and Bill & Mickey drive the rhythm section with that unmistakable 1982 swing.\n\nFor listeners wanting the clearest window into this night, the C. Miller‑remastered soundboard (gd1982-04-09.sbd.miller.32716.sbeok.flac16) is the pick: excellent board clarity, full stereo separation and no audible drop‑outs, capturing both the band’s fire and the auditorium’s ambience. Whether you’re after the Bird Song sparkle or the heartfelt Brokedown Palace finale, this recording lets you hear a truly locked‑in Dead show.",
